@@s001 Pure functions
@@s002 <p>The same list <b>1, 2</b> twice: on the left in Java, on the right in Scala. The squares are the names, the circles the cells of the list.</p>
@@s003 <p>Java: <code>a.add(5)</code> <b>modifies</b> the list that already exists. Anyone holding <code>a</code> now sees 1, 2, 5.</p><p>And the value of the call? <code>void</code>: there is nothing to replace it with.</p>
@@s004 <p>Scala: <code>Cons(5, a)</code> creates <b>a new cell</b> holding 5, which points to the list <code>a</code>. The result is a value: the new list, which I call <code>b</code>.</p>
@@s005 <p><code>a</code> has not been touched: it is still <b>1, 2</b>. The cells 1 and 2 are <b>shared</b> between <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>.</p><p>Sharing is safe only because nobody will ever be able to modify them.</p>
@@s006 dato:program A,due:program B,ok:same result,no:different result
@@s007 ora:the function,no:side effect,ok:pure function
@@s008 ok:referentially transparent,no:not RT
@@s009 dato:driver (main program),due:cluster nodes,no:lost update,ok:pure version
@@s010 Pure functions
@@s011 A function is <b>pure</b> if you can replace the call with its result without changing anything in the program. The whole course rests on this idea: that is why <code>var</code>, loops and exceptions are forbidden.
@@s012 The idea: substitute
@@s013 Take <code>square(3)</code>. It is worth <code>9</code>. If everywhere in the program you write <code>9</code> instead of <code>square(3)</code>, the program does exactly the same thing. The expression is said to be <b>referentially transparent</b> (<i>referentially transparent</i>, RT).
@@s014 A pure function: substituting changes nothing
@@s015 Program A: I calculate once and use twice.
@@s016 Program B: in place of <code>x</code> I write the call directly.
@@s017 Every <code>square(3)</code> is worth 9, always.
@@s018 Same result: <code>square</code> is pure.
@@s019 An impure function: substituting changes the result
@@s020 <code>next</code> reads and modifies an external variable. It is a <b>side effect</b>.
@@s021 Program A, starting from <code>counter = 0</code>: <code>x</code> is worth 1.
@@s022 Result of program A.
@@s023 Program B, again starting from 0: the first call gives 1, the second finds <code>counter</code> already at 1 and gives 2.
@@s024 I only substituted <code>x</code> with its definition and the result changed: <code>next()</code> is <b>not</b> referentially transparent.
@@s025 square(3) yes, next() no
@@s026 The two definitions
@@s027 Referential transparency
@@s028 An expression <code>e</code> is referentially transparent if substituting <code>e</code> with its value, wherever it appears, does not change the behaviour of the program.
@@s029 Pure function
@@s030 A function <code>f</code> is pure if <code>f(e)</code> is referentially transparent for every referentially transparent expression <code>e</code>.
@@s031 In practice a function is pure if it has <b>no side effects</b>. A side effect is everything it does beyond computing the result:
@@s032 reading or writing variables, files, streams;
@@s033 modifying a data structure "in place" or assigning a field of an object;
@@s034 throwing an exception or stopping the program with an error;
@@s035 printing or drawing on screen;
@@s036 drawing a random number.
@@s037 A practical advantage, from the slides: pure code declares everything it needs in its parameters, so it is easy to test and to replace with fakes ("mocks").
@@s038 Everything a function does beyond the result
@@s039 Java and Scala: adding an element
@@s040 The slide compares <code>a.add(5)</code> in Java with <code>Cons(5, a)</code> in Scala. Look at what happens in memory.
@@s041 Modifying against building
@@s042 Poll of the lecture
@@s043 Which of these expressions, in Java, are referentially transparent? For each one the question is always the same: can I replace it with its value, wherever it appears, without changing the behaviour of the program? Of the six on the slide only <code>f(f(x))</code> with f pure is. The others contain an assignment (<code>a = a + 42</code>, <code>z = z + …</code>), a read of mutable state (<code>a[x]</code>), a print (<code>println</code>) or an exception (<code>throw</code>): all side effects. The animation takes them one at a time with the reason.
@@s044 The six expressions of the poll, with the reason
@@s045 Why effects are banned here
@@s046 The slide uses Apache Spark, which distributes the computation over several computers. With a variable this happens:
@@s047 A counter in a distributed programme
@@s048 I want to sum the data by updating a variable. It seems harmless.
@@s049 The function <code>x => counter += x</code> is sent to every node <b>together with a copy</b> of <code>counter</code>.
@@s050 Every node increments its copy. The one in the main program stays at 0.
@@s051 Pure version: no variable. Spark can divide the work as it likes because each piece is a calculation without effects.
@@s052 At the exam
@@s053 In the exercises and at the exam: no <code>var</code>, no side effects, no exceptions, no <code>return</code>, unless explicitly asked. The complete list with examples is in <a href="../esame/regole.html">Rules and prohibitions</a>.
@@s054 In English
@@s055 "An expression is referentially transparent if it can be replaced by its value without changing the behaviour of the program. A function is pure if calling it on referentially transparent arguments is always referentially transparent, which in practice means it has no side effects."
@@s056 A counter in Spark, and the pure version
